# 2. Reachable k-decision regions

Fix a finite state/parameter set X.

For a channel

\[
\kappa:X\rightsquigarrow Y
\]

and an integer \(k\ge1\), define

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal R_k(\kappa)
=
\{
\kappa\lambda:
\lambda:Y\rightsquigarrow[k]
\}.
}
\]

Thus \(\mathcal R_k(\kappa)\) is the set of all \(k\)-action stochastic decision channels obtainable after observing \(\kappa\).

It is a compact convex subset of the stochastic-matrix polytope

\[
K(X,[k]).
\]

It contains every constant-row channel, because the observer can ignore the observation.

It is closed under downstream post-processing:

\[
A\in\mathcal R_k(\kappa),\quad
\tau:[k]\rightsquigarrow[\ell]
\]

implies

\[
A\tau\in\mathcal R_\ell(\kappa).
\]

---

# 3. Exact Blackwell representation by the full decision system

Define the decision system of \(\kappa\) as

\[
\mathcal R(\kappa)
=
(\mathcal R_k(\kappa))_{k\ge1}.
\]

### Theorem CW-001.1 — Reachable-decision representation of the Blackwell order

For finite channels \(\kappa,\mu\),

\[
\boxed{
\kappa\succeq_B\mu
\iff
\mathcal R_k(\mu)\subseteq\mathcal R_k(\kappa)
\quad\forall k.
}
\]

In fact it is enough to take

\[
k=|\operatorname{out}(\mu)|.
\]

### Proof

If

\[
\mu=\kappa\Lambda,
\]

then every decision derived from \(\mu\),

\[
\mu\Gamma,
\]

equals

\[
\kappa(\Lambda\Gamma),
\]

so

\[
\mathcal R_k(\mu)\subseteq\mathcal R_k(\kappa)
\]

for every \(k\).

Conversely let \(k=|\operatorname{out}(\mu)|\). Since the identity post-processing belongs to the admissible \(k\)-decision rules,

\[
\mu\in\mathcal R_k(\mu).
\]

If

\[
\mathcal R_k(\mu)\subseteq\mathcal R_k(\kappa),
\]

then

\[
\mu=\kappa\Lambda
\]

for some stochastic \(\Lambda\).

Thus the full family \(\mathcal R(\kappa)\) is an exact operational representation of a Blackwell-equivalence class.

