# 4. Coherent decision systems

Define a **coherent decision system** on finite X to be a family

\[
C=(C_k)_{k\ge1}
\]

such that:

1. each \(C_k\subseteq K(X,[k])\) is nonempty, compact, and convex;
2. every constant-row \(X\to[k]\) channel belongs to \(C_k\);
3. downstream closure holds:
   \[
   A\in C_k,\ \tau:[k]\rightsquigarrow[\ell]
   \Rightarrow
   A\tau\in C_\ell.
   \]

Order coherent systems componentwise by inclusion:

\[
C\preceq D
\iff
C_k\subseteq D_k
\quad\forall k.
\]

Every genuine statistical channel gives such a system through \(\mathcal R(\kappa)\).

---

# 5. Exact completed meet

Take any family of coherent decision systems

\[
(C^{(i)})_{i\in I}.
\]

Define

\[
\boxed{
\left(\bigwedge_i C^{(i)}\right)_k
=
\bigcap_i C_k^{(i)}.
}
\]

### Theorem CW-001.2 — Arbitrary meets exist

The componentwise intersection is again a coherent decision system and is the greatest lower bound.

### Proof

- compactness and convexity survive arbitrary intersections of closed subsets of the compact stochastic polytope;
- nonemptiness survives because every system contains all constant-row decision channels;
- post-processing closure survives intersection;
- the universal lower-bound property is immediate from inclusion.

Therefore coherent decision systems form a complete meet-semilattice.

Because they form a closure system inside the product of powersets, arbitrary joins also exist as the smallest coherent system containing the union.

Hence:

\[
\boxed{
\text{coherent decision systems form a complete lattice.}
}
\]

This gives a literal exact completion in which the missing Blackwell meet always exists.

