# 7. Exact representability criterion for a genuine Blackwell meet

### Theorem CW-001.3 — Blackwell meet iff the completed meet is channel-realizable

For finite channels \(\{\kappa_p\}_{p=1}^P\), the following are equivalent:

1. a greatest common Blackwell lower bound \(\mu\) exists;
2. there is a channel \(\mu\) such that
   \[
   \boxed{
   \mathcal R_k(\mu)
   =
   \bigcap_p\mathcal R_k(\kappa_p)
   \quad\forall k.
   }
   \]

### Proof: 2 ⇒ 1

Immediate from Theorem CW-001.1.

### Proof: 1 ⇒ 2

Let \(\mu\) be the greatest common lower bound.

Certainly

\[
\mathcal R_k(\mu)
\subseteq
C_k^\cap.
\]

Now take arbitrary

\[
A\in C_k^\cap.
\]

Viewed itself as a channel

\[
A:X\rightsquigarrow[k],
\]

A is a post-processing of every \(\kappa_p\), so A is a genuine common Blackwell lower bound.

By maximality of \(\mu\),

\[
\mu\succeq_B A.
\]

Therefore

\[
A\in\mathcal R_k(\mu).
\]

Hence

\[
C_k^\cap\subseteq\mathcal R_k(\mu).
\]

Equality holds for every k.

### Consequence

The Blackwell non-lattice phenomenon is exactly:

\[
\boxed{
\text{failure of the coherent common decision system }
C^\cap
\text{ to be representable by one genuine channel.}
}
\]

This is stronger and more operational than saying merely "the intersection of two zonotopes need not be a zonotope."

