# 8. Bertschinger–Rauh example as the k=2 shadow

For k=2, a stochastic decision channel is determined by one vector of probabilities, and

\[
\mathcal R_2(\kappa)
\]

is equivalent to the zonotope

\[
Z_\kappa.
\]

Bertschinger and Rauh use

\[
\kappa_1=
\begin{pmatrix}
1/3&2/3&0\\
0&1/3&2/3\\
2/3&0&1/3
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\kappa_2=
\begin{pmatrix}
2/3&1/3&0\\
0&2/3&1/3\\
1/3&0&2/3
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

Their intersection has vertices

\[
0,\quad
(5,2,2)/6,\ (2,5,2)/6,\ (2,2,5)/6,
\]

\[
(1,4,4)/6,\ (4,1,4)/6,\ (4,4,1)/6,
\quad 1.
\]

Its convex hull has triangular two-dimensional facets.

Every face of a zonotope is a zonotope, and a two-dimensional zonotope is centrally symmetric; a triangle is not.

Therefore

\[
C_2^\cap
\]

is not the two-decision region of any genuine channel.

By Theorem CW-001.3, no genuine Blackwell meet can exist.

Thus the published zonotope obstruction is exactly the binary-decision projection of the more general coherent-system representability failure.

